  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday and Sunday: Another day of widespread Red Flag \nconditions are in store for much of the Inland Northwest today \nas a shortwave trough races through the region. Overall, expect \nwest-southwest winds around 10-20 with gusts around 18-25 mph in\nmost locations under the Red Flag Warning. The one exception \nwill be the Cascade gaps where wind gusts in the evening will be\naround 25-40 mph. Fires in Chelan and Okanogan County will be \nwell ventilated with westerly flow aloft and will have favorable\nweather conditions for significant growth through the afternoon\nand evening hours. Smoke output from these fires may knock \ntemperatures down a few degrees in eastern WA and north ID, \nreducing fire risk slightly. Needless to say, avoid any \nactivities that may cause sparks as fuels are near/at record \ndryness levels for this time of year. HRRR near surface smoke \noutput is showing impressive concentrations in the evening and \novernight hours downwind in the eastern third of WA and north \nIdaho. If this were to occur, air quality will continue to be \ndegraded. Another push of Cascade gap winds will occur Sunday \nafternoon and evening as another shortwave traverses the region,\nleading to elevated to locally critical fire weather conditions\nin the Wenatchee area and Waterville Plateau with gusts around \n20-35 mph and relative humidity around 18-25 percent. The \nwildcard is smoke reducing temperatures in those areas, which \nwould reduce fire risk. \n\nMonday through Saturday: Zonal flow will continue through\nTuesday, bringing near normal temperatures and breezy winds to \nthe Cascade gaps in the evenings. Changes approach Wednesday as \nour first chance of precipitation in a few weeks returns near \nthe Canadian border as a broad shortwave trough approaches from \nthe north. There is a 10-20% chance of showers (very little \nprecipitation expected) as this system moves through. There is a\nsmall chance (10-20%) of thunderstorms in northeast WA and \nnorth ID as this system slowly departs Thursday. Ensembles are \nhinting towards above normal temperatures returning late next \nweek but with troughing setting up off the coast this raises \nconcerns for thunderstorms. /DB",
